// The two hashes a case key is built from, both 64-bit and both defined by
// their own arithmetic alone, so a key is the same on every machine and every
// run: bin/idc keeps a case's pass under its key across builds.
//
// dg_fnv is FNV-1a over the bytes of a string, continuing from dgh, so a
// token's kind and text can be folded into one running hash. The offset basis
// is left to the caller (0 here): what matters is that equal input gives an
// equal hash, and that a one-byte edit anywhere changes it.

dg_fnv(word dgh, string dgs) {
  int dgi = 0;
  while(charat(dgs, dgi) >= 0) {
    dgh = (dgh ^ charat(dgs, dgi)) * 1099511628211;
    dgi = dgi + 1;
  }
} return word dgh;
(0, "a"):(106652627936467)
(5, "ab"):(95554157542382666)

// Two 64-bit values mixed into one, the multiply-xorshift finaliser shape:
// every input bit reaches every output bit, which a plain sum or xor of two
// hashes does not do. A component's hash is a SUM of mixed values
// (../../scc/seal/), so the order its callees are found in cannot matter, and
// mixing each term first is what keeps that sum from being a weak one. dga is
// multiplied before dgb joins it, so the mix is not symmetric: (x, y) and
// (y, x) -- a function's own hash and its callees', say -- do not collide.
dg_mix(word dga, word dgb) {
  word dgx = (dga * 6364136223846793005) ^ dgb;
  dgx = dgx ^ ushr(dgx, 31);
  dgx = dgx * 1099511628211;
} chain (word dga, word dgb) {
  dgx = dgx ^ ushr(dgx, 29);
} return word dgx;
